HTTPS lifecycle assessment
Lines225–329 enforce single-use startup, locked cancellation/error choice and no session invalidation while a task is being created. Cancellation before read starts clears the buffer and completes the independent join latch; late read throws the latched failure before creating any session. The earlier deadline-preflight cleanup gap is closed at 305–313: the successful claimant releases its unstarted buffer and completes the join. It cannot clear another read invocation's live state.

Started requests cancel their captured task. Task completion produces the locked result and clears the accumulator, then requests finishTasksAndInvalidate. Session/task identities and once-only continuation extraction reject obsolete/repeated callbacks. Both completion and invalidation must be observed; continuation/join signalling is queued behind the completing delegate callback. The queue-tail closure retains only the completion latch and signal, not the request. Framework cancellation/invalidation calls, observers and continuation resumes occur outside the request lock, avoiding inversion with the deadline watchdog. The sender acquires the same result lock after a Void wake-up. Cancellation and deadline are rechecked before success publication.

The immediate weak-nil assumption has been removed. The test keeps immediate logical owner-cleanup verification, then observes an actual deinit event after a scoped strong-owner helper returns, bounded by an independent watchdog. This tests release of the delegate ownership cycle without promising synchronous Foundation/autorelease/async-frame destruction.

Shared close and provisional finding closure
HTTPS-CLOSE-01 is SOURCE CLOSED on these bytes. JPIPTransport registers each request owner with a unique ID and captured generation before suspension. close retires the generation, captures/cancels owners and clears retired state before its first await. It leaves registry entries until each sender's own defer, so duplicate closes can join the same retiring owners. HTTPS success and error paths reject an obsolete generation before telemetry/path/response publication. Old close performs no shared-state mutation after awaiting; old sender cleanup removes only its own ID. Later requests may use the replacement generation.

JPIPRequestCompletion is an idempotent mutex-protected broadcast. Waiters register or observe finished under the same mutex; finish resumes them outside it. It never consumes the sender's Result. Thus close can await completion independently of transport actor publication. For HTTPS the signal follows task completion/invalidation callback tail. For HTTP the worker callback follows finite I/O and locked result publication; it precedes continuation resume/physical thread return. Neither proves OS-thread exit nor destruction of every result reference retained by a stale sender. Opaque getaddrinfo/system DNS may delay HTTP completion; no universal close-latency guarantee follows.

